Under that broad everyday meaning—“dried plant forage a horse chews on”—yes, horses can get most or all of their calories from what people casually call straw.

The important caveat is that this broad category includes **hay and other forage**, which can be nutritionally adequate. Horses can build and maintain large muscles on forage because:

- they eat a lot of it, often 1.5–2.5% of body weight per day as dry matter;
- good grass/legume hay contains protein, energy, and amino acids;
- hindgut microbes ferment fiber into usable energy;
- their large body size and genetics are suited to herbivory.

Humans cannot live similarly on dried grass because our digestive tract does not ferment cellulose efficiently enough to extract much energy from it, and we cannot eat the necessary volume.

So the disagreement is mostly terminology: if “straw” means any dried forage, your observation is fair. If it means the specific leftover grain stalk material, it is not a complete horse diet on its own.
